<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Finlandisation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Finlandisation is the term, coined in West German debates of the 1960s and 1970s, for the policy by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> kept its democracy and market economy after 1944 while avoiding anything that might provoke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Rooted in the treaty of 1948 and practised by Presidents Paasikivi and Kekkonen, it involved neutrality, close trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and self-censorship in press and politics, and critics used the word for any small state's deference to a powerful neighbour.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finlandisation.jpg" alt="Finlandisation" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suomi_n%C3%B6yryytet%C3%A4%C3%A4n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Finlandization means the process by which a powerful country makes a smaller neighbour refrain from opposing its foreign policy while the smaller state keeps its nominal independence and its own political system. The word arose in the West German political debate of the late 1960s and 1970s. It was coined in scholarly debate and made known by the political scientists Richard Löwenthal, Walter Hallstein and Kurt Birrenbach, who feared the effects of a withdrawal of American troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Franz Josef Strauss and others used it in response to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willy-brandt">Willy Brandt</a>'s attempts to normalise relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>. It is often considered pejorative.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> had fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1939, losing 9 per cent of its territory, and re-entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> in 1941 beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. After the Paris Peace Treaty of 1947 it kept democracy and parliamentary government under heavy Soviet pressure. Its foreign relations followed the doctrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juho-kusti-paasikivi">Juho Kusti Paasikivi</a>, who stressed a trusting relationship with the Soviet Union. In April 1948 Finland signed an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finno-soviet-treaty-of-1948">Agreement of Friendship, Cooperation and Mutual Assistance</a>, which obliged it to resist attacks by Germany or its allies but also recognised its wish to stay outside great-power conflicts. Finland stayed out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a>.</p><p>Between 1944 and 1946 the Soviet part of the Allied Control Commission demanded that libraries withdraw more than 1,700 books as anti-Soviet, and Finnish censors banned films such as The Manchurian Candidate. Possession of such books was never banned, and the censorship never took the form of purging; much of it was self-censorship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> reacted in varied ways to the term. Many ordinary Finns found it offensive, while the cartoonist Kari Suomalainen defined it as the art of bowing to the East without mooning the West.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolved in 1991, the treaty of 1948 was replaced by a new treaty with the Russian Federation on a more equal footing, ending the Paasikivi-Kekkonen doctrine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 1995. After the Russian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 2022, Finland applied for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> membership on 18 May and became a member on 4 April 2023. The word has also been applied to other states near a great power, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s attitude toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> between 1871 and 1940 and Swiss policy toward Nazi Germany.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Finlandization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1944 President Juho Kusti Paasikivi and his successor Urho Kekkonen, president from 1956 to 1982, pursued neutrality combined with careful friendship with Moscow under the treaty of 1948, a policy critics abroad called <b>Finlandisation</b>; Kekkonen’s long rule concentrated great power in the presidency.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
